1. Create a Cloudflare account

Go to:

Cloudflare

A free account is sufficient.

You do not need to move your domain or DNS to Cloudflare for this setup. The Worker can have its own *.workers.dev address.

2. Create a D1 database

In the Cloudflare dashboard:

Workers & Pages → D1 SQL Database → Create

Give it a name such as:

page-counter

Once created, open the database and go to the SQL editor.

Run:

CREATE TABLE page_views (
    day TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
    count INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0
);

That's the entire database.

It will eventually contain something like:

day	count
2026-08-22	31
2026-08-23	47
2026-08-24	12

So we aren't storing information about visitors — just a number for each day.

3. Create a Worker

Go to:

Workers & Pages → Create → Worker

Give it a name, for example:

my-page-counter

Cloudflare will give you an address similar to:

https://my-page-counter.yourname.workers.dev

Open the Worker and choose Edit code.

Replace the generated code with:

export default {
  async fetch(request, env) {
    // Only allow GET requests
    if (request.method !== "GET") {
      return new Response("Method not allowed", { status: 405 });
    }

    // Today's date in Belgian time
    const day = new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en-CA", {
      timeZone: "Europe/Brussels",
      year: "numeric",
      month: "2-digit",
      day: "2-digit"
    }).format(new Date());

    // Increment the counter and then read the new value.
    await env.DB.prepare(`
      INSERT INTO page_views (day, count)
      VALUES (?, 1)
      ON CONFLICT(day)
      DO UPDATE SET count = count + 1
    `).bind(day).run();

    const result = await env.DB
      .prepare("SELECT count FROM page_views WHERE day = ?")
      .bind(day)
      .first();

    return new Response(
      JSON.stringify({
        date: day,
        count: result.count
      }),
      {
        headers: {
          "Content-Type": "application/json",
          "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": "*"
        }
      }
    );
  }
};

There's one important part left: env.DB needs to be connected to your D1 database.

4. Connect the Worker to D1

In your Worker settings, find:

Settings → Bindings

Add a D1 database binding.

Use:

Variable name:

DB

And select the D1 database you created:

page-counter

The variable name must be DB, because that's what the JavaScript above uses:

env.DB

Save/deploy the Worker.

5. Test it

Open your Worker URL directly in your browser:

https://my-page-counter.yourname.workers.dev

You should get something like:

{
  "date": "2026-08-24",
  "count": 1
}

Refresh the page:

{
  "date": "2026-08-24",
  "count": 2
}

Refresh again:

{
  "date": "2026-08-24",
  "count": 3
}

🎉 You now have a working counter.

6. Put it on your GitHub Pages website

Suppose you currently have something like:

<body>

    <h1>My Website</h1>

    <button onclick="toggleText()">Show text</button>

    <p id="text" style="display:none;">
        Hello!
    </p>

</body>

Add somewhere on the page:

<p>
    Today's visitors: <span id="visitor-count">...</span>
</p>

Then add this JavaScript:

<script>
async function updateVisitorCount() {
    try {
        const response = await fetch(
            "https://my-page-counter.yourname.workers.dev"
        );

        const data = await response.json();

        document.getElementById("visitor-count").textContent = data.count;
    } catch (error) {
        console.error("Could not load visitor count:", error);
    }
}

updateVisitorCount();
</script>

Replace:

https://my-page-counter.yourname.workers.dev

with your actual Worker URL.

Now your GitHub Pages website can display:

Today's visitors: 37

Every time someone loads the page, the Worker increments today's counter.

One thing I'd change depending on what you mean by "today"

I've deliberately made the counter use Belgian time (Europe/Brussels), rather than UTC.

So if someone visits at:

23:59

and another person visits at:

00:01

the two visits belong to different days according to your local time.

You can change that if you'd rather have the counter reset at midnight UTC.

Important limitation

This counts page loads, exactly as you requested.

So if I:

open your website → +1
refresh → +1
refresh again → +1

that's 3 visits.

It does not try to determine whether those were three different people.

Also, because the counter endpoint is public, someone could deliberately call it thousands of times and inflate the number. For a personal/small website that's probably fine, but if the number is important, we'd want to add some basic abuse protection.

Cost

For a small site, this should genuinely remain within Cloudflare's free limits. The current Workers Free plan allows 100,000 requests/day, while D1's free allowance includes 100,000 rows written/day.

So you can have your GitHub Pages + HTML/CSS/JS + daily public counter without running or paying for a traditional server.

If you want, I can also give you an even simpler version that requires no database at all, using a free counter service — but the Cloudflare/D1 version above gives you much more control and keeps your actual count under your own Cloudflare account.
